{"ts": 1789779825.7842848, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Full sweep complete. The picture is very clean and worth interpreting carefully before choosing.\n\n**Structure of the signal.** F1 alone gives 1.261. F27 alone gives 0.789. Together they give 1.446 \u2014 a real diversification gain, and F27+F4 (0.947, vs 0.789 and 0.556 singly) confirms F27 carries independent signal rather than being a shadow of F1. Every other feature, when added to F1, *reduced* Sharpe. That is the signature of a panel where two features are genuinely informative and the other 38 are mostly noise: adding noise to an equal-weight combination dilutes the signal-to-noise ratio mechanically, since each feature gets weight 1/k regardless of merit.\n\n**The third-feature question.** Adding a third feature to {1,27} produced a best of 1.501 (F4), with F11 at 1.494 and F13 at 1.491. This is the part that demands skepticism. I searched ~38 candidates for that third slot. Under the null that all third features are pure noise, the *maximum* of 38 draws will land well above zero by construction \u2014 and the spread of outcomes I observed (1.18 to 1.50) is exactly the dispersion you'd expect from noise, with the winners clustered tightly at the top. The gain from 1.446 to 1.501 is +0.055 Sharpe, which is far smaller than the selection-induced upward bias from a 38-way max. F4 was the best single third feature *and* had the best standalone Sharpe of the remainder, which is mildly reassuring, but three different features land within 0.01 of each other at the top \u2014 a pattern that reads as noise ties rather than one true signal.\n\nThe honest read: the third slot buys me roughly nothing real and costs me an overfitting penalty. I'll take **{F1, F27}**.\n\nLet me do a couple of sanity checks on sign robustness before submitting."}
