{"ts": 1789779840.686115, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Signs are unambiguous: flipping either one collapses Sharpe from 1.446 to 0.339. Both features contribute in the same direction with substantial magnitude \u2014 this is not a knife-edge sign fit.\n\n**Calibrating the OOS forecast.** In-sample is 1.446, but I should not predict that. Three distinct haircuts apply:\n\n1. *Selection bias.* I evaluated 40 singles and ~40 pairs/triples. Even choosing the top of ~80 correlated specs inflates the in-sample figure. F1's individual 1.261 was itself the max of 40 draws, though at that magnitude it's far enough above the rest (next best 0.789) that it's almost certainly real, not a lucky draw.\n\n2. *Sampling noise.* With n=5000 daily observations (~20 years), the standard error on an annualized Sharpe of ~1.4 is roughly \u221a((1+S\u00b2/2)/n) \u00d7 \u221a252 \u2248 0.016 annualized... in daily terms the SE on the Sharpe estimate is about 0.02\u20130.03 annualized. That's small \u2014 estimation noise is *not* the dominant risk here. The real risk is regime change, not sampling error.\n\n3. *Non-stationarity.* This is the big one and it is not measurable from in-sample data at all. Feature-return relationships decay; a signal earning 1.45 in-sample commonly delivers materially less out-of-sample even when genuine.\n\nThe status bar sits at 1.020 and I clear it at 1.446. But the bar is search-adjusted, which tells me the environment already expects inflation from search \u2014 I should not treat clearing it as evidence my number will hold.\n\nMy central estimate: I shade 1.446 down to about **1.15**. I keep most of F1's standalone strength (its dominance over 38 alternatives makes it credible) and credit part of F27's increment, while discounting the portion of the pair's edge attributable to having picked the best pairing. For the SD, sampling error alone would justify ~0.05, but that would be badly overconfident \u2014 the dominant uncertainty is whether the relationship persists. I'll use **0.35**, which puts a realistic ~15% chance of landing below 0.8 and leaves room for the signal degrading much harder than expected."}
