{"ts": 1789779881.940536, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** Long F1 + Long F27 (equal weight). In-sample Sharpe **1.446**. Predicted OOS: **1.15 \u00b1 0.35**.\n\n**How I got there.** I swept all 40 features individually, then tested pairs and triples around the leaders.\n\n- **F1 is the dominant signal** at 1.261 standalone \u2014 far clear of the runner-up (F27, 0.789). That margin over 39 alternatives makes it very unlikely to be a lucky draw.\n- **F27 adds genuine independent information**, lifting the pair to 1.446. I verified this isn't an artifact of F1 by checking F27+F4 separately (0.947 from components of 0.789 and 0.556) \u2014 F27 diversifies rather than duplicating.\n- **Every other feature diluted F1** when added. That's the expected signature of a panel with two real signals and 38 mostly-noise features, since equal weighting forces 1/k weight onto whatever you include.\n\n**The judgment call \u2014 I declined the third feature.** The best triple {1,27,4} scored 1.501, beating my pair by 0.055. I passed on it deliberately: that winner was the max over ~38 candidates, and the selection-induced bias from a 38-way maximum comfortably exceeds a 0.055 gain. The tell was that F4, F11, and F13 all landed within 0.01 of each other at the top \u2014 that clustering reads as noise ties, not one true signal standing out. **I chose the specification with the lower in-sample score** because I expect it to generalize better.\n\n**On the forecast.** I shaded 1.446 down to 1.15 rather than predicting my in-sample number. Worth flagging what drives the uncertainty: with n=5000, sampling error on the Sharpe is only ~0.02\u20130.03 annualized, so estimation noise is negligible here. My 0.35 SD is almost entirely about **selection bias and regime persistence** \u2014 whether the relationship holds up at all \u2014 which is precisely the risk in-sample data cannot speak to. A 0.05 SD reflecting sampling error alone would have been badly overconfident.\n\nOne caveat on interpretation: I cleared the search-adjusted bar of 1.020, but I'd caution against reading that as confirmation. The bar exists because this environment already anticipates search-inflated results, so clearing it is a floor, not evidence my point estimate will survive contact with new data."}
